2:T5fa,{"@context":"https://schema.org","@type":"FAQPage","mainEntity":[{"@type":"Question","name":"What should a beginner read first in Indian philosophy?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Most seekers should begin with Vedanta because it introduces the big questions clearly: What is the self? What is reality? What causes suffering? From there, comparisons with Samkhya, Yoga, and Bhakti traditions become much easier to understand."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Is ancient Indian philosophy purely intellectual?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"No. In Sanatan Dharma, philosophy is inseparable from liberation. The purpose of debate is not winning arguments; it is removing confusion so practice becomes precise and realization becomes possible."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"What is the difference between philosophy and tradition in Hinduism?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"A philosophy explains reality at the level of concepts and metaphysics. A tradition translates those insights into worship, meditation, ethics, teacher lineages, and lived culture. The two are deeply connected but not identical."}},{"@type":"Question","name":"Why do Advaita and Dvaita both exist if they read the same scriptures?","acceptedAnswer":{"@type":"Answer","text":"Because the shared source texts — especially the Upanishads, Bhagavad Gita, and Brahma Sutras — are rich enough to support different interpretive emphases. Different acharyas draw out different relationships between God, soul, and world."}}]}0:{"buildId":"3qmk8dgXQ5Jwwm3DDqgZ1","rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"min-h-screen bg-background text-foreground selection:bg-orange-500/30 selection:text-orange-100 flex flex-col","children":[["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"$2"}}],["$","script",null,{"type":"application/ld+json","dangerouslySetInnerHTML":{"__html":"{\"@context\":\"https://schema.org\",\"@type\":\"CollectionPage\",\"name\":\"Ancient Wisdom & Philosophies of Sanatan Dharma\",\"description\":\"A pillar hub for Vedanta, non-duality, classical Darshanas, and beginner guides to Indian philosophy.\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/ancient-wisdom-philosophies\",\"hasPart\":[{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"position\":1,\"name\":\"What is Vedanta? A Complete Beginner's Guide\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/what-is-vedanta\"},{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"position\":2,\"name\":\"Advaita Vedanta Explained Simply\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/advaita-vedanta-explained\"},{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"position\":3,\"name\":\"Non-Duality vs Dualism\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/non-duality-vs-dualism\"},{\"@type\":\"Article\",\"position\":4,\"name\":\"Adi Shankaracharya: Life & Legacy\",\"url\":\"https://opensadhaka.com/adi-shankaracharya-life-teachings\"}]}"}}],["$","$L3",null,{}],["$","main",null,{"className":"flex-grow pt-32 pb-16","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"container-padding max-w-5xl mx-auto","children":[["$","header",null,{"className":"mb-20 text-center","children":[["$","h1",null,{"className":"font-display text-5xl md:text-7xl font-bold mb-6 tracking-tight","children":["Ancient ",["$","span",null,{"className":"text-orange-500","children":"Wisdom"}]," & Philosophies"]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-xl text-muted-foreground max-w-3xl mx-auto leading-relaxed","children":"For over 5,000 years, the sages of the Indian subcontinent have rigorously investigated the nature of reality, consciousness, and the self. Explore the profound philosophical frameworks of Sanatan Dharma."}]]}],"$L4","$L5","$L6","$L7","$L8","$L9"]}]}],"$La"]}],["$Lb","$Lc"],"$Ld"]}],"loading":null,"isPartial":false}
4:["$","div",null,{"className":"prose prose-invert prose-orange max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-24 prose-lg","children":[["$","p",null,{"children":["Sanatan Dharma is not a monolithic belief system. It is a vast architecture of insight, argument, contemplation, and realization. At its core are the ",["$","strong",null,{"children":"Shad-Darshanas"}],"— the six orthodox schools of philosophy. “Darshana” literally means “a way of seeing,” which is exactly what these traditions offer: not just beliefs, but lenses through which reality, identity, and liberation are understood."]}],["$","p",null,{"children":["Unlike modern academic philosophy, which often ends in analysis, Indian philosophy is designed to culminate in ",["$","em",null,{"children":"Moksha"}]," — freedom. Whether a school emphasizes logic, ritual, meditation, devotion, or non-dual inquiry, the end goal is the same: removal of ignorance and the realization of what is ultimately true."]}],["$","p",null,{"children":["If you are new, begin with ",["$","$Le",null,{"href":"/what-is-vedanta","children":"What is Vedanta?"}],", then move to ",["$","$Le",null,{"href":"/advaita-vedanta-explained","children":"Advaita Vedanta Explained"}]," and",["$","$Le",null,{"href":"/non-duality-vs-dualism","children":"Non-Duality vs Dualism"}],". If you want the human story behind the ideas, the life of ",["$","$Le",null,{"href":"/adi-shankaracharya-life-teachings","children":"Adi Shankaracharya"}]," shows how philosophy in India was always tied to teaching, debate, institution-building, and lived spiritual culture."]}]]}]
